---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AffiliateBox from '../components/AffiliateBox.astro';
import DisclosureBlock from '../components/DisclosureBlock.astro';

/**
 * COMPARISON PAGE TEMPLATE (3–5 products, one clear top pick).
 * Ad-free by policy: this page's job is to help readers choose — mixing
 * ads into it violates AdSense policy on affiliate-heavy pages.
 */
---
<BaseLayout
  title="The 5 Best Sleep Supplements (2026)"
  description="We compared the 5 sleep supplements with the strongest research support: magnesium glycinate, glycine, L-theanine, melatonin, and ashwagandha. One clear top pick."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Comparison</span>
      <h1>The 5 Best Sleep Supplements, Honestly Compared</h1>
      <p class="article-meta">By <a href="/author">Fahim</a> · Updated September 2026</p>
    </header>

    <DisclosureBlock />

    <div class="quick-answer">
      <h2>Short on time?</h2>
      <p>
        For general sleep quality: <strong>magnesium glycinate</strong>, 200–400 mg before bed —
        gentle, well-studied, and the best value. For stress-driven trouble:
        <strong> ashwagandha</strong>. For a racing mind: <strong>L-theanine</strong>.
        For jet lag only: <strong>low-dose melatonin</strong> (0.5–1 mg, not 10).
        The reasoning and full comparison are below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most "best sleep supplement" lists are copy-paste marketing. This one is different:
        we only include supplements with multiple human trials behind them, we tell you
        what the trials actually measured, and we name one clear starting pick instead of
        hedging with five "winners."
      </p>

      <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Supplement</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Evidence strength</th>
            <th>Typical dose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Magnesium glycinate</strong></td>
            <td>Overall sleep quality; most people</td>
            <td>Moderate</td>
            <td>200–400 mg</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2. Glycine</td>
            <td>Falling asleep faster</td>
            <td>Moderate (small trials)</td>
            <td>3 g</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>3. L-theanine</td>
            <td>Racing mind at bedtime</td>
            <td>Moderate</td>
            <td>100–200 mg</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4. Melatonin</td>
            <td>Jet lag, shifted schedules</td>
            <td>Strong (for timing issues)</td>
            <td>0.5–1 mg</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>5. Ashwagandha</td>
            <td>Stress-driven sleep trouble</td>
            <td>Moderate</td>
            <td>300–600 mg extract</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The top pick: magnesium glycinate</h2>
      <p>
        If we had to pick one supplement for general sleep quality, it's magnesium glycinate.
        Magnesium plays a role in regulating the nervous system's "quiet down" signals, and the
        glycinate form is gentle on the stomach (unlike citrate, which is better known as a
        laxative). Trials on magnesium and sleep show modest but consistent improvements in
        sleep quality — modest is honest; anyone promising more is selling you something.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AffiliateBox
      badge="Top Pick"
      name="Magnesium Glycinate (via iHerb)"
      slug="magnesium-glycinate"
      blurb="Well-absorbed, gentle form. Pick a third-party tested brand — we link the category so you can compare prices and reviews yourself."
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Also excellent</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Glycine (3 g before bed)</strong> helped participants fall asleep faster and feel
        more rested in small Japanese trials. <strong>L-theanine</strong> is the pick if your
        problem is a racing mind rather than physical restlessness. <strong>Melatonin</strong> is
        genuinely strong for jet lag and shift work — it shifts sleep <em>timing</em>, it doesn't
        knock you out; most people take 5–10x more than the effective dose. <strong>Ashwagandha</strong>
        earns its place when stress is the root cause — see our full
        <a href="/articles/ashwagandha-for-stress-what-the-research-says">ashwagandha deep-dive</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How we evaluate</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Human trials only.</strong> Rat studies and petri dishes don't count.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple studies.</strong> One encouraging trial is a lead, not a conclusion.</li>
        <li><strong>No disease claims.</strong> Supplements support sleep; they don't treat insomnia disorder. If you have chronic sleep problems, see a doctor.</li>
        <li><strong>Safety first.</strong> We flag interactions (ashwagandha and thyroid medication, melatonin and blood thinners, and so on).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Bottom line</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the basics — consistent sleep schedule, dark room, no late caffeine — then, if
        you want a supplement, magnesium glycinate is the most sensible first try. Give any
        supplement 2–4 weeks before judging it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AffiliateBox
      badge="Stress Pick"
      name="Ashwagandha KSM-66 (via iHerb)"
      slug="ashwagandha"
      blurb="The most-studied extract (KSM-66). Not for pregnant/nursing readers or those on thyroid meds without a doctor's OK."
    />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
